===Notable drops===
===={{plink|Swift albatross feather}}====
Swift albatross feathers are used to create a [[gale catcher]], an upgraded version of the [[wind catcher]].
{{Drop sources|Swift albatross feather}}

===={{plink|Ray barbs}}====
Ray barbs are used for creating high-level [[trawling nets]].
{{Drop sources|Ray barbs}}

===={{plink|Echo pearl}}====
Echo pearls are used for building a [[fathom pearl]], which helps locate [[shoals]] when [[deep sea trawling]].
{{Drop sources|Echo pearl}}

===={{plink|Narwhal horn}}====
Narwhal horns can be crafted into a [[narwhal horn knife]], which can be used as a regular [[knife]] when [[fletching]], or built into a [[chum spreader]].
{{Drop sources|Narwhal horn}}

===={{plink|Inky paint}}====
Inky paint is a cosmetic [[boat paint]] trimming the boat with a black colour.
{{Drop sources|Inky paint}}

===={{plink|Bottled storm}}====
Bottled storms are used for building a [[greater teleport focus (facility)|greater teleport focus]], allowing players to use the [[Teleport to Boat]] spell.
{{Drop sources|Bottled storm}}

===={{plink|Broken dragon hook}}====
Broken dragon hooks are used for building a [[dragon salvaging hook]].
{{Drop sources|Broken dragon hook}}

===={{plink|Dragon nails}}====
Dragon nails are used for building a number of [[core boat parts]] and [[facilities]].
{{Drop sources|Dragon nails}}

===={{plink|Dragon metal sheet}}====
Dragon metal sheets are used for building a number of [[core boat parts]] and [[facilities]].
{{Drop sources|Dragon metal sheet}}

===={{plink|Dragon keel parts}}====
Dragon keel parts are used for building a [[dragon keel]].
{{Drop sources|Dragon keel parts}}

===={{plink|Large dragon keel parts}}====
Large dragon keel parts are used for building a [[dragon keel]].
{{Drop sources|Large dragon keel parts}}

==Combat mechanics==
[[File:Stop following captain's target.png|alt=Depicts the UI button for the "Stop following Captain's target" order|thumb|Stop following Captain's target]]
Players or crew members can man cannons, as long as the crewmate's privateering level is at or above the privateering level of the player's cannon. Fortunately, a ship can use two [[rune cannon|rune]] or [[dragon cannon]]s, as there are two crew members with 4 Privateering; [[Oarswoman Olga]] and [[Jittery Jim]].  

Players must have one free inventory space to hold cannonballs, as these are stackable, or they can store the cannonballs on their boat's cargo hold (they will be used automatically). A [[ballistic attractor]] can be built on the player's boat to recover 60% of cannonballs fired.

Provided the player's boat is positioned in a multiway combat area, both cannons may be fired upon separate targets by using the "Stop following Captain's target" order for the cannon being operated by a crewmate. Once a crewmate has been ordered to fire upon a target, issuing the "Stop following Captain's target" order frees the player or another crewmate to attack a different target without interrupting the first crewmate's combat order. Alternatively, player can use the "Fire at will" order, which will make crewmates target any attackable monsters within their line of sight. After 10 minutes, crewmates will take a break from firing at will and resume following captain's target. To make them fire at will again, player must give the order again, and clear their target as well. If player just uses the "Fire at will" order, but doesn't clear the target, crewmates will still try to target whatever captain is targeting, even if captain's target is out of their line of sight. 

It should also be noted that if the target is positioned straight on either the front or back end of the ship, cannons from both sides can reach it. Players may also “wiggle” their boat, rotating it slightly every few ticks so that each cannon re-enters its firing arc on the 5-tick firing cycle. This allows both cannons to fire every 5 ticks without needing to align the monster head-on, although care should be taken not to click on the sail.

===Cannons===
A [[Cannon_(sailing)|cannon]] can fire all types of cannonballs at or below its own tier. When fired by a crewmate, a cannon's max hit is determined by the player's visible [[Sailing]] level and the combined strength bonus of the cannon and cannonballs being fired. Additionally, the max hit is scaled down by a factor based on the crewmate's [[Privateering]] level. Based on in-game testing, the crewmate max hit appears to use the following formulas:

<math>
\text{Base max hit} = \Bigg\lfloor\frac{\text{Sailing level} \times (\text{Cannon strength} + \text{Cannonball strength} + 64) + 320}{640}\Bigg\rfloor + \text{Perildance boost}
</math>

<math>
\text{Crewmate max hit} = \Bigg\lfloor\text{Base max hit} \times \frac{\text{Privateering level} + 13}{20}\Bigg\rfloor 
</math>

When fired by a player, the player's visible [[Ranged]] level is used instead of their Sailing level, and the max hit is unaffected by any crewmates. In both cases, visible skill boosts and drains can affect the max hit.

Having a [[keg of perildance bitter]] on the boat will increase the cannon's max hit by one (before the crewmate scaling, if applicable). 

{{Calculator:Sailing/Cannon max hit}}

=== Defences ===
Ships have health, a defence level, a ship armour value and 7 defence bonuses (stab, slash, crush, light, standard, heavy, magic). The [[keel]] is responsible for the ship armour value while the [[hull]] sets the ship's defence level as well as its defence bonuses. The ship total health is the addition of keel and hull health values.

Ship armour reduces incoming damage by 1 for every 100 ship armour point, so having a better keel will greatly decrease damage. Monsters with max hits below the armour value will be unable to land any hit at all, essentially making the player's ship invincible against them. A [[dragon keel]] makes the player's ship invincible against all but a few high-level monsters.

See {{Section|Monsters}} for the max hit of sea monsters against ships with various keels installed.

===Ship manoeuvering===
If a player has a crewmate, either the player or the crewmate can move the ship around the monster to mitigate raft/boat damage. This only works if the monster's primary attack is melee. Players must stay within the monster's aggressive range; otherwise, the monster will stop attacking the player. If the player's raft or boat is critically damaged, players can evade combat with a [[wind-catcher]], making the ship sail faster and eventually escaping the monster.

===Ship repair===
Ship [[repair kits]] can recover the boat's health during or after combat, depending on the type of repair kit used on the ship. 

Crewmates may also repair the ship using the [[repair kits]] stored in the ship's [[cargo hold]]. They provide an increased heal of their deckhandiness level − 1, which is added to the repair kit’s base healing. Commanding a crewmate to repair the ship does not give the player any [[attack delay]]. 

{{Boat Repair Kit Stats}}

