{{Infobox Scenery |name = Shooting Star Noticeboard |image = [[File:Shooting Star Noticeboard.png|280px]] |release = [[17 March]] [[2021]] |update = Shooting Stars |members = Yes |quest = No |location = [[Observatory]] |options = Read |examine = A good place to keep track of shooting star activity. |map = {{Map|name=Shooting Star Noticeboard|x=2443|y=3189|r=3|mtype=square}} |id = 41230 }} The '''Shooting Star Noticeboard''' can be found behind the [[Observatory assistant]] in the reception building, which is to the North of the [[Observatory]].
Reading it displays the last five [[Crashed Star]]s that have landed on that [[world]], along with the name of the player who was the first to mine it. The amount of stars the player has personally found is tracked here as well.
[[File:Shooting Star Noticeboard read.png|thumb|left|300px|Reading the noticeboard.]] {{Shooting Stars}}

{{External|rs=Shooting Star}} {{Infobox Activity |name = Shooting Stars |image = [[File:Shooting Stars.png|300px]] |release = [[17 March]] [[2021]] |update = Shooting Stars |type = Distraction and Diversion |members = No |location = Global |skills = [[Mining]] |currency = [[Stardust]] |music = Varies on location of star |map = {{Map|zoom=-1|x=3290|y=3353|width=400|align=right|group=Stars|caption=A map of landing sites.}} |leagueRegion = General }}
'''Shooting Stars''' are a [[Distraction and Diversion]] in which a meteoroid falls from the sky approximately every hour and a half (90 minutes) across every in-game [[server]]. These appear as [[Crashed Star]]s that can be mined for [[stardust]], which members can exchange at [[Dusuri's Star Shop|a shop]] run by [[Dusuri]] at the [[Mining Guild]] entrance in [[Falador]].
On every world, a shooting star will fall at a random spot selected from a predetermined list roughly every 90 minutes (with a variation of up to 15 minutes), although stars on different worlds may fall closer in time to one another, or even at the same time.
Although the activity is available in [[free-to-play]] worlds, players only get awarded half the experience points. On top of that, [[Dusuri's Star Shop]], where players can exchange stardust for rewards, is [[members]] only.
==Locating== [[File:Shooting Stars - telescope view.png|thumb|300px|A shooting star viewed through a telescope.]] Members can use a [[Telescope space|telescope]] in a [[Study|study room]] in a [[player-owned house]] to find the approximate area and time of the next landing. It is also possible to use telescopes in another player's house for this purpose. * If the telescope is blocked by another room in the house, (not including outdoor rooms such as the [[Menagerie]]), or the room is underground, it will not work. * Better telescopes provide a more accurate time window: ** [[Oak telescope]] - 24-minute window ** [[Teak telescope]] - 9-minute window ** [[Mahogany telescope]] - 2-minute window * If a star has recently fallen, the following text appears: ** "You look through the telescope but you don't see anything interesting." Join the Shooting Stars [[grouping]] chat via the grouping menu to share locations with other players.
There are also many websites available on the internet which list known star sites allowing players to quickly find stars meeting their criteria.
If a player is standing near the crash site where a shooting star is falling, they will hear a whistling sound as it falls from the sky and the crash as it impacts the ground.
==Mining== [[File:Shooting Star crashing.gif|frame|A shooting star crashing.]] [[Crashed Star]]s fall in sizes from 6 to 9. Players can find out how large the star is by prospecting it. Additionally, prospecting it will reveal the progress to the next level. {{Listen |title = Crashed Star discovered |align = right |filename = Star of Your Own (Shooting Stars).ogg |desc = The jingle that plays upon discovering a crashed star.}} The first person to attempt to mine the crashed star will be notified in the chatbox that they are the first to find the star, alongside a [[jingle]]. This person also will receive double the stardust for the next 300 they mine as a reward. (Note: This bonus stardust does not need to come from the same star that granted the bonus, and seems to persist after logging out or a server update). In addition, their name will be added to the [[Shooting Star Noticeboard]] in the reception room north of the [[Observatory]]; up to five players are displayed on the noticeboard. If another crashed star is discovered while still having a remaining bonus from a previous discovery, the previous bonus will be lost and the new bonus will be set to 300.
Each layer of the star is mined from 0% to 100%, at which point the outermost layer sloughs off, and the star's size is reduced by one tier. The amount left to mine of the current layer can be determined by prospecting while mining. Each layer will '''always''' deplete at a fixed rate of 7 minutes, up to a maximum of 63 minutes for a size 9 star, provided there is at least one player mining it.
Players will receive [[stardust]] as they mine the crashed star, receiving 32 experience each time a piece of stardust is mined (16 experience on Free-to-play worlds; 32.8 with full [[Prospector kit|Prospectors]]). A roll for mining stardust is performed regularly based on the player's [[Mining]] level, scaling from 29% to 46% at levels 1 to 99. [[Mining enhancers]] that multiply yield do '''not''' work on crashed stars.
Players may receive a pet [[rock golem]] whilst mining, where the base chance is 1/521,550.
Once the final layer (when the star is size one) is fully mined, the star will crumble and disappear. Stars will also disappear if they have not been fully mined once a new star crashes within the same world.{{CiteTwitter|author=Mod Ash|url=https://twitter.com/JagexAsh/status/1392938928341200901|date=13 May 2021|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20210513202538/https://twitter.com/JagexAsh/status/1392938928341200901|archivedate=13 May 2021|quote=[do shooting stars reset if not being mined?] Looks to me like an old star would be deleted at the time when the next spawns, if it hasn't already gone.}}
===Star sizes=== {| class="wikitable" style="text-align:center" !Size !{{SCP|Mining}} Level !Crash chance |- |9 || 90 || 20% |- |8 || 80 || 25% |- |7 || 70 || 30% |- |6 || 60 || 25% |- |5 || 50 || {{NA}} |- |4 || 40 || {{NA}} |- |3 || 30 || {{NA}} |- |2 || 20 || {{NA}} |- |1 || 10 || {{NA}} |}
===Mining Success Chance=== {{Skilling success chart|color1=goldenrod|high1=117|image1=Stardust_175.png|label=Stardust mining chance|label1=Stardust|low1=73|req1=10|showbefore=no}}
===Notes=== *The shooting star cycle will reset during a system update; stars on the ground before the update will vanish. Stars will begin landing again 20–50 minutes after the game goes back online, and then cycle as normal (every ~90 minutes) *It is possible that the telescopes of two POH will show different timers. In such a case, the landing time can be narrowed down to the time when the timers overlap. For example, if one telescope says a star is 10-12 minutes from landing and another says 9-11 minutes, the star must be 10-11 minutes from landing. *Only one star can crash per given world at a time. Any existing stars will despawn when the next star falls, even if it is still being mined. *The experience given for mining stardust is halved in free-to-play. *The [[adamant pickaxe]]'s 4-[[tick]] cycle allows it to be used for [[tick manipulation]] while mining shooting stars, unlike other pickaxes. *Gems are able to be obtained from mining stars. A charged [[amulet of glory]] will increase this rate. A [[gem bag]] may be worth bringing, or a [[chisel]] to craft [[bolt tips]]. *In [[Varlamore]], crashed stars can reward the [[Loop half of key (moon key)]]. * The check for remaining time on a star occurs earlier in the mining script than the check for the required level. As a result, clicking on a star can cause it to degrade up to 1% per 7 ticks. Clicking multiple times does not impact the amount of degradation. This does not stack with other people mining/etc.
== Money making == {{Mmgsection|Mining crashed stars}}
==Rewards== {{Main|Dusuri's Star Shop}} [[Stardust]] is obtained by mining the [[crashed star]]. Stardust is used as currency at [[Dusuri's Star Shop]] to purchase the [[celestial ring]] along with other rewards.
{| class="wikitable align-center-1" |+Dusuri's Star Shop ! colspan="2" |Reward !Price sold at !Price bought at |- |{{plinkt|Celestial ring#Uncharged|pic=Celestial ring (uncharged)|txt=Celestial ring (uncharged)}} | {{Currency|Stardust|2000}} |{{Currency|Stardust|1600}} |- |{{plinkt|Star fragment}} |{{Currency|Stardust|3000}} |{{Currency|Stardust|2400}} |- |{{plinkt|Bag full of gems#Stardust|pic=Bag full of gems (stardust)|txt=Bag full of gems}} | {{Currency|Stardust|300}} |{{Currency|Stardust|240}} |- |{{plinkt|Soft clay pack#Stardust|pic=Soft clay pack|txt=Soft clay pack}} |{{Currency|Stardust|150}} |{{Currency|Stardust|120}} |} Experience rates vary greatly based on level, the tier of pickaxe used, and time spent locating stars. If mining consistently with a [[Dragon pickaxe]] and 90+ mining, players can expect approximately 30,000 experience per hour.
