* [[Dramen Staff]] / Elite [[Lumbridge & Draynor Diary]] * Teleport jewellery * [[Royal seed pod]] * [[Master scroll book]] * [[Morytania legs 3]] or above * [[Chronicle]] * [[Teleport crystal]] / [[Eternal teleport crystal]] * [[Teleport to house (tablet)]] / [[construct. cape]] / [[max cape]] * [[Perfected quetzal whistle|Quetzal whistle]] * [[Mythical cape]] after DS2 quest * Max POH with [[Portal nexus]] and [[rejuvenation pool]] * Coins for charter ships * [[Gem bag]] * [[Rune pouch]] with teleport runes * [[Brown apron]] to gain access inside the [[Crafting Guild]]
==Landing sites== Members may make use of a [[Telescope space|telescope]] to reveal the general area in which the star will fall, wherein there are a number of set locations in which the star may land. A star will not land in the same spot twice in a row.{{CiteTwitter|author=Mod Ash|url=https://twitter.com/JagexAsh/status/1469848696179376128|date=12 December 2021|archiveurl=https://reldo.runescape.wiki/i/100596|archivedate=25 April 2023|quote=If I'm reading the code correctly, it rolls randomly from a list that *excludes* this Darkmeyer spot. And if it rolls the same place it used before, it'll instead go to this spot.}}
The only way to locate the actual crash site is to search each possible crash site in the given area until the [[Crashed Star|crashed star]] is found; this process, as well as the mining process, can be greatly expedited with the assistance of other players.
There are a total of 82 possible landing sites, 21 of which are located in free-to-play areas. Stars may land in members areas on free-to-play worlds.
Some crash site areas contain aggressive mobs; low level players should take care when selecting a star location to mine at, especially if they do not intend to pay attention while mining. Star locations contained within cities are typically safe.
