==Overhearing the workers to the south==
* '''Shipyard Worker (A):''' They say a good way of remembering it is that a ship can have boats on it, but a boat can't have ships on it.
* '''Shipyard Worker (B):''' But say you have a ship with lifeboats on it, and that ship starts to sink. If all the lifeboats are deployed, it no longer has any boats on it, so is it no longer a ship?
* '''Shipyard Worker (A):''' Er...
* '''Shipyard Worker (B):''' I suppose if it's sinking it probably no longer matters. Still, at the moment it does sink, you could argue that it very briefly stops being a ship and starts being a submarine.
* '''Shipyard Worker (A):''' Very briefly.
* '''Shipyard Worker (B):''' Indeed.
* '''Shipyard Worker (A):''' It's not just boats and ships that have this problem. You also have orcas.
* '''Shipyard Worker (B):''' More commonly known as the killer whale!
* '''Shipyard Worker (A):''' Except by many definitions, they're not really whales at all.
* '''Shipyard Worker (B):''' Yeah...
* '''Shipyard Worker (B):''' What is it with things at sea being named so poorly?
* '''Shipyard Worker (A):''' To be honest, it's not much better on land. Think about trees. You've got oak trees, maple trees, and then you just have... trees.
* '''Shipyard Worker (B):''' 'What's your boat made of?' 'Wood.' 'So like oak or...?' 'No, just wood.'
* '''Shipyard Worker (A):''' The joys of language.
* '''Shipyard Worker (B):''' The joys indeed.
* {{tact|end}}

